    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The flow behavior of emulsions of Prudhoe Bay Crude oil both in water and in brine through capillary tubes at room temperature and at low temperatures is described analytically and verified experimentally. A unique technique of preparing high quality emulsions using small amounts of surfactants was developed. Along with this \"combined emulsification\" technique, the optimum conditions for preparing emulsions, as determined through investigation of homogenization type and time as well as emulsifier type and concentration has been presented. Rheological characterization of emulsions was carried out with a newly developed \"variable head capillary rheometer\". The complete description, operation and working equations of the instrument are presented.Particle size and particle size distribution were determined using a Model B Industrial Coulter Counter before and after the flow tests.The \"power law model\" with its constitutive equations formed the basis for the quantitative description of the experimental results. For the entire range of shear stress and shear rate investigated at both temperatures, emulsions up to and in some cases including 60 percent quality (volumetric concentration of oil in the emulsion) were found to be Newtonian. Above this and up to 90 percent quality, the emulsions were characterized by non-Newtonian, pseudoplastic behavior.The effect of surfactant type and concentration (0.10, 0.30, 1.00 Ibs of Triton X-114 per barrel of oil and 0.056, 0.084 and 0.140 Ibs of NaOH per barrel of oil) on rheological behavior of the emulsions were also investigated at both temperatures.The emulsion droplet diameters ranged from 10 to 150 microns, and were found to follow a log-normal distribution. The effect of homogenization time, surfactant type and concentration, quality and the shear on particle size and particle size distribution was investigated and is also presented.Based on these studies, 75 to 80 percent Prudhoe Bay crude oilin-brine emulsions stabilized by 1. 0 Ib/bbl of Triton X-114 concentration appear to be the best of those studied here for use in a cold dispersion pipeline in the arctic.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
